            <div xml:id="d525-a1">

                <pb facs="0817" n="689" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg689"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 312.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Williams to Mr. Davis</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Havana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1885-02-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 13, 1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 118.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the dispatch, No. 115,
                    addressed to the Department under date of the 11th instant, by Mr. Vickers, our
                    consul at Matanzas, with regard to the fine of $2,000 imposed upon the American
                    barkentine Ocean Pearl, of Portland, Me., by the customs authorities of
                    Matanzas, for the non-delivery of ten empty hogsheads, from her last cargo
                    brought from Philadelphia, I now beg to inclose, for the use of the Department,
                    the sworn statement, dated the 10th instant, and made before Consul Vickers by
                    E. S. Henley, master of the said barkentine.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>RAMON O. WILLIAMS,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
